[
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "Alright, we just got a call from a listener named Marcus in Portland, and he's asking about something a lot of people are curious about right now — why mortgage rates move when the Fed meets. Marcus, thanks so much for calling in to Audexa Radio. This is a great question, and I think a lot of our listeners are wondering the same thing.",
    "type": "listener_request"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "It really is, Alex. So here's the thing — a lot of people think the Fed directly controls mortgage rates, but it's actually a bit more nuanced than that. The Fed sets what's called the federal funds rate, which is the interest rate that banks charge each other for overnight loans.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "Okay, so they're not literally setting my mortgage rate?",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Exactly. But here's where it connects — when the Fed raises or lowers that federal funds rate, it sends a signal to the entire financial market about where interest rates are heading. Mortgage lenders look at that signal, and they adjust their rates accordingly because they're anticipating future economic conditions.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "So it's like they're reading the room based on what the Fed is doing?",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Exactly right. Plus, mortgage rates are also tied to longer-term bond yields, especially the 10-year Treasury. When the Fed signals it's going to keep rates higher for longer, investors get nervous about bonds, and those yields go up — which pushes mortgage rates up too.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "So when we hear the Fed is meeting, people in the real estate world are basically holding their breath waiting to see what happens?",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Absolutely. And that's why you'll see mortgage rates sometimes move *before* the Fed actually makes an announcement. The market is already pricing in what it thinks the Fed will do. If the Fed surprises everyone and does something different, then rates can swing pretty dramatically.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "Marcus, hopefully that clears it up for you. The bottom line is the Fed doesn't set your mortgage rate, but what they do definitely influences it. Thanks so much for the call, and thanks for listening to Audexa Radio.",
    "type": "listener_request"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Great question, Marcus. If anyone else out there has questions about how the Fed impacts your wallet, keep those calls coming. We love tackling this stuff.",
    "type": "transition"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "Stick around — after the break, we're diving into some wild earnings reports from the tech sector. You won't want to miss it. Stay tuned to Audexa Radio.",
    "type": "transition"
  }
]
```

