[
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "Alright, we've got a really cool call coming in right now on Audexa Radio. This is from Marcus in Portland, and Marcus, you want to know about how black holes actually form. I love this question — it's wild stuff. Jordan, are you ready to dive into some cosmic science?",
    "type": "listener_request"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Absolutely, and Marcus, great question. So here's the thing — black holes form when massive stars reach the end of their lives. We're talking about stars that are at least 20 times more massive than our sun.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "Twenty times bigger? That's huge. So what happens to them?",
    "type": "listener_request"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Right, so when these massive stars run out of fuel, they collapse. And I mean they collapse catastrophically. All that material — we're talking about an enormous amount of mass — gets squeezed down into an incredibly small space. It's called a supernova explosion, and it's one of the most violent events in the universe.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "So the star just... implodes on itself? That's insane.",
    "type": "listener_request"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Exactly. And when you compress that much mass into such a tiny area, gravity becomes so intense that nothing can escape from it — not even light. That's what defines a black hole. The boundary where light can't escape is called the event horizon.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "Wait, so if light can't escape, how do we even know black holes exist? I mean, they're invisible, right?",
