  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Great follow-up. So we can't see them directly, but we can see their effects. Black holes pull in material from nearby stars and gas clouds. As that material spirals in, it heats up and releases energy — X-rays, radiation — and that's what telescopes can detect. Plus, we can observe how stars and gas orbit around them, even though we can't see the black hole itself.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "So it's like detecting an invisible force pulling things in. Okay, I've got one more for you — are black holes just floating around out there, or are they connected to galaxies?",
