    "text": "Wait, so he didn’t just invent a technique — he proved that editing could manipulate how we feel? That’s wild for the silent film era!",
    "type": "listener_request"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Exactly. Kuleshov took a neutral expression of the famous actor Ivan Mozzhukhin and cut it to three different shots: a bowl of soup, a girl in a coffin, and a woman on a divan. Audiences swore the actor looked hungry, grief-stricken, or lustful — even though his face was identical each time. It revealed that meaning isn’t just in the image; it’s in the cut.",
    "type": "listener_request"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "So it’s not just about what you show — it’s about what you show next. That’s the foundation of modern editing, right? Like, every time we feel something in a movie because of how it’s cut, we’re feeling the Kuleshov effect?",
    "type": "listener_request"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Precisely. It’s why montage works — think of Rocky’s training sequence, or the shower scene in Psycho. The emotion isn’t in the individual shots; it’s built in the juxtaposition. Kuleshov’s insight laid the groundwork for montage theory, which Eisenstein later expanded into intellectual montage, but it all starts with that simple, powerful idea: context is created by editing.",
    "type": "listener_request"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "It’s kind of amazing that a film school experiment from over a century ago still shapes how we watch movies today. I’ll never look at a reaction shot the same way again.",
    "type": "listener_request"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "And that’s the beauty of it — once you know about the Kuleshov effect, you start seeing it everywhere. It’s not just film; it’s in TV, ads, even TikTok edits. The way we sequence images to evoke emotion? That’s Kuleshov’s legacy.",
    "type": "listener_request"
  },
