    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Exactly. But here's the thing—the actor's face didn't change at all. The emotion was created entirely by the juxtaposition of the images. It proved that meaning in cinema isn't just in the shot itself, but in how you cut between them.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "Mind-blowing. So basically, editing creates the narrative, not just the acting? That sounds like the foundation of almost every movie we watch today.",
    "type": "transition"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Spot on. This idea became the cornerstone of Soviet Montage theory. Directors like Eisenstein took it further, using conflict between shots to generate intellectual responses rather than just emotional ones. It shifted film from being a recorded play to its own unique art form.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
