  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "I am just glad my old split-screen opponents could not reprogram the console mid-match. Now, speaking of gaming relics getting a massive overhaul, let's turn our attention over to Jagex.",
    "type": "transition"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "Huge news for MMO veterans: Jagex officially announced they are developing a fourth mainline RuneScape installment, and for the first time in franchise history, it is being built in Unreal Engine. On top of that, they are partnering with creator MoistCr1tikal for an official animated series.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "RuneScape in Unreal Engine feels completely surreal to say out loud. Millions of us grew up clicking willow trees in a web browser on a crusty school library PC. What does jumping to a modern powerhouse engine mean for a game historically built on tile grids and point-and-click combat?",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "That is the central dilemma. Old School RuneScape thrives precisely because of its low-spec accessibility, tick-based mechanics, and charming simplicity. Unreal Engine allows for stunning lighting, real-time action combat, and expansive vertical world design. But if Jagex strips away the rhythmic, low-stress comfort of RuneScape's core identity, they risk alienating the loyal audience that sustained them for over twenty years.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "Right, nobody wants RuneScape to become another generic high-fantasy action game. That actually ties into a great essay sparking debate on Hacker News this week titled \"City building games have a soul problem.\"",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "It strikes at the exact same design question. The argument is that modern city builders—like Cities: Skylines 2—have become hyper-focused on traffic flow algorithms, road grading, and supply chains, but they have lost the whimsical charm of classic SimCity. Older games gave you quirky news tickers, bizarre mayoral approval ratings, and distinct neighborhood personalities.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "Yes! Give me back the personality! If my citizens aren't complaining about llama ordinances or scrambling because a giant robot stomped down Main Street, what are we doing here? Sometimes deeper mathematical simulation does not automatically translate to more joy.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host2",
    "text": "It is the trap of hyper-realism. When studios prioritize fidelity and massive technical scale above all else, they often leave less room for handcrafted whimsy and memorable emergent storytelling. Developers across every genre should really pay attention to that feedback.",
    "type": "deep_dive"
  },
  {
    "speaker": "host1",
    "text": "Could not agree more. For everyone tuning in across the country, what gives a game its soul for you? Send us your thoughts on the Audexa text line. Don't go anywhere—we'll be right back after this track.",
    "type": "outro"
  }
]

